Here are 25 of the most prolific and influential science fiction writers, spanning the Golden Age, New Wave, and contemporary eras, along with their key contributions:

The Golden Age & Foundational Masters
Isaac Asimov

Key Works: Foundation series, Robot series (I, Robot).

Legacy: Authored and edited over 500 books across his career, defining core concepts like the Three Laws of Robotics and Psychohistory.

Robert A. Heinlein

Key Works: Stranger in a Strange Land, Starship Troopers, The Moon Is a Harsh Mistress.

Legacy: Often called the "dean of science fiction writers," known for hard sci-fi, social commentary, and introducing adult themes to the genre.

Arthur C. Clarke

Key Works: 2001: A Space Odyssey, Rendezvous with Rama, Childhood's End.

Legacy: One of the "Big Three" of hard sci-fi alongside Asimov and Heinlein; famously conceptualized satellite communications.

Ray Bradbury

Key Works: Fahrenheit 451, The Martian Chronicles, The Illustrated Man.

Legacy: Blended poetic prose with speculative fiction, exploring dystopian surveillance and human philosophy.

Philip K. Dick

Key Works: Do Androids Dream of Electric Sheep?, Ubik, The Man in the High Castle.

Legacy: Published 44 novels and 121 short stories centered on paranoia, altered reality, and artificial consciousness.

Jack Williamson

Key Works: The Legion of Space, The Humanoids.

Legacy: A career spanning over 75 years, credited with coining the terms terraforming and genetic engineering.

Mid-Century, New Wave & Space Opera
Robert Silverberg

Key Works: Dying Inside, Lord Valentine's Castle, Downward to the Earth.

Legacy: A Grand Master with dozens of novels and hundreds of short stories, pivoting from pulp to deeply psychological New Wave sci-fi.

Harlan Ellison

Key Works: "Repent, Harlequin!" Said the Ticktockman, "I Have No Mouth, and I Must Scream".

Legacy: Edited the ground-breaking Dangerous Visions anthology and published over 1,700 short stories, screenplays, and essays.

Michael Moorcock

Key Works: The Dancers at the End of Time, Jerry Cornelius series.

Legacy: A central figure in the British New Wave movement as editor of New Worlds, credited with popularizing the concept of the Multiverse.

Brian W. Aldiss

Key Works: Non-Stop, Helliconia trilogy, Supertoys Last All Summer Long.

Legacy: Wrote over 40 novels and 30 short story collections, helping bridge science fiction into mainstream literary appreciation.

Poul Anderson

Key Works: Tau Zero, The Broken Sword, Dominic Flandry series.

Legacy: Seven-time Hugo Award winner who published over 80 books across hard sci-fi, space opera, and historical fantasy.

Frederik Pohl

Key Works: Gateway (Heechee Saga), The Space Merchants.

Legacy: Active in the industry for over seven decades as a writer, magazine editor, and literary agent.

Ursula K. Le Guin

Key Works: The Left Hand of Darkness, The Dispossessed, The Lathe of Heaven.

Legacy: Revolutionized the genre by exploring anthropology, feminism, anarchism, and sociology through spec-fic worlds.

Anne McCaffrey

Key Works: Dragonriders of Pern series, The Ship Who Sang.

Legacy: The first woman to win a Hugo Award for fiction, famous for blending science fiction worldbuilding with planetary fantasy.

Samuel R. Delany

Key Works: Babel-17, Dhalgren, Triton.

Legacy: Known for dense, avant-garde explorations of language, sexuality, and social structures.

Philip José Farmer

Key Works: Riverworld series, World of Tiers series.

Legacy: A pioneer in introducing explicit sexual themes and complex, interconnected mythologies to mainstream pulp sci-fi.

Modern Masters & Expanding Universes
C. J. Cherryh

Key Works: Downbelow Station, Cyteen, Foreigner series.

Legacy: Author of over 80 novels, praised for exceptionally detailed political and linguistic alien perspectives.

Lois McMaster Bujold

Key Works: Vorkosigan Saga (Shards of Honor, A Civil Campaign).

Legacy: One of the most awarded authors in speculative fiction, tying Heinlein for the most Best Novel Hugo Awards (4).

Alan Dean Foster

Key Works: Humanx Commonwealth series, Pip and Flinx series.

Legacy: Extremely prolific original worldbuilder and film novelizer (Star Wars, Alien, Star Trek).

Ben Bova

Key Works: Grand Tour series (Mars, Venus, Jupiter).

Legacy: Former editor of Analog and author of over 120 books, focusing heavily on human expansion through the solar system.

Iain M. Banks

Key Works: The Player of Games, Use of Weapons, Excession (Culture series).

Legacy: Revitalized space opera with his sprawling, utopian post-scarcity AI society.

Peter F. Hamilton

Key Works: The Reality Dysfunction (Night's Dawn trilogy), Pandora's Star (Commonwealth Saga).

Legacy: Known for massive, multi-threaded epic space operas that span thousands of pages.

Neal Stephenson

Key Works: Snow Crash, The Diamond Age, Cryptonomicon, Seveneves.

Legacy: Popularized terms like metaverse and heavily influenced modern cyberpunk, virtual reality, and speculative tech.

Orson Scott Card

Key Works: Ender's Game, Speaker for the Dead, Homecoming series.

Legacy: First author to win both the Hugo and Nebula awards for Best Novel in consecutive years (Ender's Game and Speaker for the Dead).

Alastair Reynolds

Key Works: Revelation Space series, House of Suns, Chasm City.

Legacy: A former European Space Agency astrophysicist who brought authentic hard science and dark, gothic scales to modern space opera.